1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:12,000
If you've scrolled TikTok lately, you've probably seen "secret menu" coffee orders at Starbucks or Dunkin' - a dozen pumps of syrup, extra drizzle, and two or three flavor shots stacked into one cup.

2
00:00:12,320 --> 00:00:17,760
Some of these videos get millions of views, but the sugar hiding in them deserves a closer look.

3
00:00:18,080 --> 00:00:29,040
One widely covered order was reported to pack in more than 20 teaspoons of added sugar in a single iced coffee - more than what's in a large soda, just dressed up as coffee.

4
00:00:29,360 --> 00:00:33,120
Three things make these drinks worse for blood sugar than people expect.

5
00:00:33,440 --> 00:00:42,240
First, sugar dissolved in liquid hits your bloodstream faster than sugar in solid food, since there's no fiber or fat slowing digestion down.

6
00:00:42,560 --> 00:00:49,600
Second, stacking multiple syrups and flavor pumps makes it easy to lose track of just how much sugar you're actually adding.

7
00:00:49,920 --> 00:00:59,760
Third, caffeine itself can blunt insulin sensitivity for a few hours, so the spike from all that sugar tends to run higher and last longer on top of it.

8
00:01:00,080 --> 00:01:02,320
None of this means coffee is the problem.

9
00:01:02,640 --> 00:01:15,440
Ask for sugar-free syrup, cut the pumps in half, or swap extra syrup for a splash of milk - and skip ordering it on an empty stomach, since food alongside it slows down how fast the sugar hits.
